  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This CaltechDATA dataset provides the real-time files, session metadata, and variable descriptions supporting a study on decoding motor plans with a closed-loop ultrasonic brain-machine interface. Its impact record is still early, but it already shows documented downstream reuse: one linked scholarly article and one associated software item reference the dataset.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2022 dataset is the companion record for a scholarly article on decoding motor plans using a closed-loop ultrasonic brain-machine interface. Archived in CaltechDATA and assigned a DOI, it packages the 2 Hz real-time data alongside session-level metadata and a guide to the variables in each file, making the underlying experimental record more usable for inspection and reuse.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest evidence of impact comes from reuse connections rather than citation volume. The record shows two documented downstream reuses: one associated article and one software item that requires the dataset. That pattern suggests the data are functioning as a bridge between the original study and follow-on analytical or implementation work.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the broader attention record is quiet. OpenAlex currently reports no citations, Datacite reports no views or downloads, and scite shows no citing evidence. Those signals indicate limited measured uptake so far, but they do not negate the dataset\u2019s value as a well-described research output supporting a specialized neurotechnology workflow.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The author list spans Caltech and collaborating institutions in France and the United States, reflecting the cross-institutional nature of the project. Overall, the dataset\u2019s contribution is best characterized as foundational infrastructure for a specific line of stem research: carefully curated experimental data that is already linked to subsequent scholarly and software reuse, even though formal citation and usage metrics remain sparse.</p>",
